      "receiptSource": "Original Receipt in \u0027\u003Ci\u003EThe Cook and Housekeeper\u0027s Dictionary\u003C/i\u003E\u0027 by Mary Eaton (\u003Ca href=\u0022references.html#eaton-1822\u0022\u003E\u003Cb\u003EEaton 1822\u003C/b\u003E\u003C/a\u003E);",
      "receiptText": "ANCHOVY PASTE. Pound them in a mortar, rub the pulp through a fine sieve, pot it, cover it with clarified butter, and keep it in a cool place. The paste may also be made by rubbing the essence with as much flour as will make a paste; but this is only intended for immediate use, and will not keep. This is sometimes made stiffer and hotter, by the addition of a little flour of mustard, a pickled walnut, spice, or cayenne.\u003Cbr\u003E"
